507	  <section class="rail-section">
508	    <div class="rail"><span class="rail-num">04</span><span class="rail-label">Grounding</span></div>
509	    <div class="content">
510	      <h2>What the sources actually say</h2>
511	      <p class="lede">Five findings, each read in full — not a title-only skim — with what it changes here.</p>
512	      <div class="findings-grid">
513	        <div class="finding feature">
514	          <h3>Spec-driven development has converged, industry-wide</h3>
515	          <p>By 2026 every major AI coding tool — GitHub Spec Kit (93K+ stars, v0.8.7), AWS Kiro, Cursor, Google Antigravity, Tessl — ships some form of spec-driven development, and they've converged on the same shape: specify → plan → tasks → implement. That's graph → canon → vector → lambda, independently arrived at.</p>
516	          <div class="foot">
517	            <a href="https://www.martinfowler.com/articles/exploring-gen-ai/sdd-3-tools.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler — Understanding SDD: Kiro, spec-kit, Tessl ↗</a>
518	            <span class="change"><b>Validates:</b> the pipeline's shape, not one agent</span>
519	          </div>
520	        </div>
521	        <div class="finding">
522	          <h3>Living specs beat static ones</h3>
523	          <p>Alignment loss during execution caused more failed AI-generated PRs than incorrect task descriptions did — the root cause is specs that stop matching the code, not bad specs. Bidirectional sync closes that gap.</p>
524	          <div class="foot">
525	            <a href="https://www.augmentcode.com/guides/living-specs-for-ai-agent-development" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Augment — Living Specs ↗</a>
526	            <span class="change"><b>Changes:</b> canon:correct-spec becomes the default response to contradiction</span>
527	          </div>
528	        </div>
529	        <div class="finding">
530	          <h3>Self-review is measurably biased</h3>
531	          <p>LLMs favor their own output in self-review. The strongest pattern adds a third role — a challenger disproving the checker's own findings, with an arbiter deciding.</p>
532	          <div class="foot">
533	            <a href="https://www.augmentcode.com/guides/adversarial-code-review" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Augment — Adversarial Review ↗</a>
534	            <span class="change"><b>Changes:</b> dimensions 01–03 stay in the checker role, never the drafting agent's own pass</span>
535	          </div>
536	        </div>
537	        <div class="finding">
538	          <h3>Impact analysis should be a graph, not a memory</h3>
539	          <p>TDAD builds an explicit dependency map between source files and tests, so before committing, the agent knows exactly which tests a change puts at risk — a queryable graph, not a checklist.</p>
540	          <div class="foot">
541	            <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.17973" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDAD paper (arXiv 2603.17973) ↗</a>
542	            <span class="change"><b>Changes:</b> insertion 01's implementer scan runs deterministically, before the agent turn</span>
543	          </div>
544	        </div>
545	        <div class="finding">
546	          <h3>Budget the working set, not the window</h3>
547	          <p>Accuracy degrades from ~50K tokens of genuinely relevant context onward, even inside much larger windows. Context editing cut consumption 84% in one 100-turn eval.</p>
548	          <div class="foot">
549	            <a href="https://foojay.io/today/context-is-a-budget-eight-levers-and-three-workflow-patterns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Context Is a Budget ↗</a>
550	            <a href="https://github.blog/ai-and-ml/github-copilot/improving-token-efficiency-in-github-agentic-workflows/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub — Token Efficiency ↗</a>
551	            <span class="change"><b>Changes:</b> scans are plain tool calls; gate artifacts show diffs only</span>
552	          </div>
553	        </div>
554	      </div>
555	    </div>
