314	    <section id="s2" class="stop">
315	      <div class="stop-head"><span class="stop-num">02</span><div><span class="stop-sub">Google · OpenAI · SST · and everyone else</span><h2>The rest of the field</h2></div></div>
316	      <p class="lede">None displace Claude Code for this repo's workflow — each is instructive differently. Four are worth a full look; a much longer constellation is worth knowing by name.</p>
317	
318	      <div class="grid">
319	        <div class="card c-amber">
320	          <h4>AGY <span class="pill">Google · closed-source</span></h4>
321	          <p class="lead">Google's Antigravity CLI — terminal surface of Antigravity, successor to the retired Gemini CLI.</p>
322	          <ul class="fact">
323	            <li>Plugin layout mirrors Claude Code: <code>plugin.json</code>, <code>skills/</code>, <code>agents/</code>, <code>hooks.json</code></li>
324	            <li>Reportedly multi-vendor (Gemini, Claude, GPT-OSS) — unconfirmed in Google's own docs</li>
325	            <li>Docs show local-path install only, no first-party remote marketplace command</li>
326	          </ul>
327	          <div class="plus"><b>Distinctive</b><span>Multi-vendor by design; shares one agent core with the Antigravity IDE</span></div>
328	          <div class="minus"><b>Caveat</b><span>Closed-source binary; thinnest verification of the group</span></div>
329	        </div>
330	        <div class="card c-amber">
331	          <h4>Codex CLI <span class="pill">OpenAI</span></h4>
332	          <p class="lead">Formal Plugin Marketplace (Mar 2026): skills + app connectors + MCP, enterprise policy gating.</p>
333	          <ul class="fact">
334	            <li>Explorer/Worker manager-subagent model, GA since March</li>
335	            <li>Community view: narrower/younger than Claude Code's hooks+MCP depth</li>
336	            <li>Seen as a more predictable "reliable executor," stabler quotas</li>
337	          </ul>
338	          <div class="plus"><b>Distinctive</b><span>Enterprise plugin policy, GPT-5.6 default, deep JetBrains reach</span></div>
339	          <div class="minus"><b>Caveat</b><span>No sanctioned path to a cheaper backend model</span></div>
340	        </div>
341	        <div class="card c-cyan">
342	          <h4>opencode <span class="pill">OSS · anomalyco</span></h4>
343	          <p class="lead">Now out-stars Claude Code itself — ~200k stars (repo moved from sst/opencode to anomalyco/opencode) vs. Claude Code's ~135k.</p>
344	          <ul class="fact">
345	            <li>75+ providers via Models.dev, plus dedicated Kimi K2 and GLM-4.7 provider integrations and Qwen3 Coder via its own Zen tier</li>
346	            <li>Mature per-agent <code>model:</code> override — the strongest option today for genuinely mixed Kimi/GLM/Qwen/Claude/GPT teams (see §05)</li>
347	            <li>Real headless story: <code>opencode serve</code> exposes an OpenAPI 3.1 server (TUI/CLI/IDE are just clients of it), plus an official GitHub Actions app triggered by PR/issue comments or cron — more built-out for remote/CI driving than Claude Code's current cloud-session model</li>
348	            <li>Ships a Claude Code compatibility shim: falls back to reading a project's <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>~/.claude/skills/</code> when its own conventions are absent — this repo's context would partially work there unmodified</li>
349	          </ul>
350	          <div class="plus"><b>Distinctive</b><span>Pay-per-use Zen pricing (sold at cost) and enterprise per-seat pricing with zero token cost on a bring-your-own gateway — different economics than subscription tiers</span></div>
351	          <div class="minus"><b>Caveat</b><span>No plugin marketplace (npm/local files only) and no native team/workflow equivalent — the one concrete PR attempting multi-agent orchestration (#32167) was closed unmerged, not just unbuilt</span></div>
352	        </div>
353	        <div class="card c-violet">
354	          <h4>Augment Code <span class="pill">enterprise · giant repos</span></h4>
355	          <p class="lead">Repositioned around retrieval quality, not chat.</p>
356	          <ul class="fact">
357	            <li>Auggie CLI + Context Engine (semantic index, not vector search) + Cosmos (team-scale orchestration) + Intent (local worktree multi-agent)</li>
358	            <li>Claims #1 SWE-bench Pro — 51.8% vs. Claude Code's 34.8% on the <i>same</i> model, credited to retrieval, not the model</li>
359	            <li>Context Engine now ships as a standalone MCP server — usable from Claude Code without switching harness</li>
360	          </ul>
361	          <div class="plus"><b>Distinctive</b><span>Worth trying alongside Claude Code for large/multi-repo retrieval — no harness switch required</span></div>
362	          <div class="minus"><b>Caveat</b><span>Sunset VS Code/JetBrains extensions (Jul 2026) — real backlash, credit-based pricing called opaque; Prism router is a closed vendor-curated pool, no open Kimi/GLM mixing</span></div>
363	        </div>
364	      </div>
365	
366	      <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.6rem;">
367	        <h4>Verdict <span class="pill">for this repo</span></h4>
368	        <ul class="fact" style="margin-bottom:0;">
369	          <li><b>opencode</b> → the pick for multi-vendor/heterogeneous-team experiments</li>
